title: Record created, updated and closed times
effort: M
area: format
created: 2026-09-17T04:17:09Z
updated: 2026-09-17T05:46:05Z

The format has no timestamps. Beads filters on created, updated and closed
and has a stale query for work untouched recently, which is exactly what an
agent supervisor wants. pidly can only answer that with git log per ticket.

Project fields rather than header lines: they do not help answer "can I start
this?", which is what the header's fixed positions are for.

Open questions. Whether closed time is needed at all, given the close is a
git commit. Whether updated is maintained by pidly or left to filesystem
mtime, which is free but does not survive a clone.

Note for whoever implements it: find on this machine is bfs, which rejects
relative -newermt arguments like '-2 minutes' and wants an ISO timestamp.
Any staleness query needs to avoid GNU-only find syntax.

Two fields, not three, both pidly's rather than the project's — valid whether
or not schema.md declares them, so an older tracker gets stamps without a
file the project owns being rewritten underneath it. ISO-8601 UTC, so
lexical order is chronological order and no date arithmetic is needed to
compare them.

created is written once by new and nothing touches it again. It is not a
copy of anything else in the file, which is the test desc: failed.

updated is bumped by every pidly command that writes to info — claim,
release, edit, reap — and by nothing else. close and reopen move a directory
without opening the file, and a hand-edit cannot bump anything, so the field
means "when pidly last wrote here". That is the lie desc: was rejected for,
taken deliberately this time: a stale desc: broke a bounded read silently,
while a stale updated makes one ticket look quiet in one query that exists
to prompt attention rather than to feed anything.

No closed field. Where a ticket is already answers whether it is closed, and
a second record can disagree — reopen would have to clear it and a hand-run
mv never would. The date of a close is the commit that made the move.

pidly list --stale 7d is what updated is for: open work pidly has not
written to in that long, oldest first, same durations as a lease. A ticket
with no stamp is listed with "-" rather than assumed fresh.

init backfills both, from git log --follow (--follow because closing is a
move, and without it every finished ticket is dated the day it finished) and
from mtime outside a repo. A ticket it cannot date keeps no line at all.
Unreadable files are skipped whole rather than rewritten.

One shell fault found, now in the design: the backfill loop ended in an
AND-list, which set -e ignores where it stands but not where its status ends
up — it became the loop's status, then the pipeline's, then init's, so every
init with nothing left to date exited 1.

The staleness note in the ticket about bfs rejecting relative -newermt
arguments did not apply in the end: nothing shells out to find for dates.
Comparison is lexical on ISO strings.

Done: bin/pidly, covered by tests/times.sh (34 cases), with new.sh and
edit.sh updated for the extra lines.
